My streak broke a few times in the last year. Had to come to terms with that.

@Paul
I find ENTREE clued as main dish to be jarring, too. I'm American, but I've lived in France for the past 4 years. Granted, restaurant life and food here is pretty important, but I'm still surprised at how fast that happened. 

Only in the US (and maybe Canada?) is entrée the main. Although I bet Quebec uses entrée to mean appetizer. I'd be interested to hear what the Canucks say.

@Paul,
Apparently, there’s legitimate history for why entree diverged in meaning as it did. Per casaschools.com, a culinary website, it occurred from the simplification of historical fine French dining where 5 or more courses were offered. At that time entree was used as the “entryway” to the heavy meat course, but there was typically a potage and fish course served prior to the entree (typically a stew, a fricassee, or a savory pie), which was then followed by something like a roast as the main course. As meals simplified over time, stews, and fish became accepted parts of the heavy, primary course. In the US, we took all of it and kept the word entree (fish, stews, savory pies, and heavy cuts of meat) probably due to hotels and fine restaurants wanting it to sound more upscale.  Elsewhere, Europe, the UK (and with UK’s influence) Australia and NZ kept entree to mean the entryway to the “heavy” course (the main or mains). 

All I can say is thank goodness for the simplification, regardless of how it is described on menus. I cannot imagine sitting for the hours it would take for 5 - 7 courses to be served.

